              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for a stalk, then the Latin for a granary."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: pecus, pecoris",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "pecus, pecoris — \"livestock, cattle, a herd of animals\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Agricola multum pecus habet: ovēs, caprās, bovēs. The farmer has a lot of livestock: sheep, goats, oxen."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Third declension, neuter: pecus, pecoris n., the animals as a group. Another word pecus, pecudis f. is one beast. Pecūnia 'money' comes from the same root: wealth was once counted in cattle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "pecus is inherited from PIE peḱu- 'livestock' (EtymDB); Lewis & Short list Gothic faihu, German Vieh and English fee as cousins. pecūnia 'money' is built on the same root (Lewis & Short)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
            }
          ]
        },
