          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "armentum, armentī — \"a herd (of cattle or horses)\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Armenta bovum in campīs sunt. Herds of cattle are on the plains."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Second declension, neuter: armentum, armentī n. Large animals, cattle and horses; grex goes with sheep and goats."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "armentum is usually linked with arō 'to plough', since Lewis & Short give its first sense as cattle for ploughing; EtymDB connects it with arma instead, so the origin is not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
