              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "pāscō, pāscere — \"to pasture, to put out to graze; to feed\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Puer caprās in colle pāscit. The boy pastures the goats on the hill."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "pāscō, pāscere, pāvī, pāstum. Passive pāscor, 'to graze': ovēs pāscuntur. Pāstor 'shepherd' comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "pāscō is inherited from PIE peh₂- 'to protect, to feed' (EtymDB); pāstor 'shepherd' and pābulum 'fodder' come from the same root (Lewis & Short). English food is a Germanic cousin."
            }
          ]
        },
